.TH WS 3
.SH NAME
ws, remoteweb \- WebSocket connections, and the site's webfs over one
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #I /net

.B /net/ws/clone
.BI /net/ws/ n /ctl
.BI /net/ws/ n /data
.BI /net/ws/ n /local
.BI /net/ws/ n /remote
.BI /net/ws/ n /status

.B remoteweb
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
On p9web,
.B #I
has one protocol,
.BR ws ,
whose connections are the browser's WebSockets.
As with
.IR ip (3),
opening
.B clone
makes a connection,
.IR n ,
and gives its
.BR ctl ;
reading
.B ctl
gives
.IR n .
The control message
.BI connect " host" ! path
opens a WebSocket to
.BI wss:// host / path\f1,
or
.B ws://
for a host on this machine, and the write returns when it is open or has failed.
.B hangup
closes it.
Each write to
.B data
is one binary message, and reads give the messages that come, a read's worth at a time;
after the far end closes, reads return 0 bytes.
.B status
gives the connection's state.
.PP
So
.IR dial (2)
reaches a 9P server over a WebSocket, and
.IR srv (4)
posts it:
.EX
	srv ws!acme.connor.zip!9p web
	mount /srv/web /mnt/web
.EE
.PP
The site serves
.IR webfs (4)
at
.BR /9p :
its requests are made by the server, not the browser, so the browser's
cross-origin rules do not stop them.
It fetches only from the hosts it allows, at first
.B api.anthropic.com
and
.BR opencode.ai ,
and holds no keys.
.PP
.I Remoteweb
mounts the site's webfs on
.B /mnt/web
in the caller's name space, dialing
.BI ws! $site !9p
and posting it as
.B /srv/web.remote
if no live connection is posted there.
The page sets
.B $site
to its own host; with no
.BR $site ,
.I remoteweb
does nothing.
.IR Sibyl 's
servers and
.IR searchfs (4)
run in a name space that it has made so.
.SH SOURCE
.B k/src/kernel/netws.ts
.br
.B k/src/web9p.ts
.br
.B site/worker.js
.br
.B rc/bin/remoteweb
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ip (3),
.IR srv (4),
.IR webfs (4),
.IR searchfs (4)
.SH BUGS
A connection that the site drops takes its mounts with it;
.I remoteweb
dials again only for mounts made after.
